    confidence: 'HIGH',
  },
];

const hiddenChargeCards = [
  {
    title: 'Fuel surcharge',
    text: 'An airline-imposed variable added on top of the base per-kg rate. It moves with fuel and is frequently buried in the quote line, which is why the all-in figure matters.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Security surcharge',
    text: 'Covers security screening and related compliance steps. Specific amounts were not published in the verified snapshot, so ask for it as a separate line.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Terminal handling',
    text: 'Origin and destination handlers charge for receiving, screening and staging the cargo. This is usually a fixed line that competitors fold into the headline rate.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Airport storage after free time',
    text: 'Air cargo gets a limited free-storage window at MCT; days beyond that become chargeable. The exact window and per-day rate are not published — request them.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Clearance & brokerage',
    text: 'Customs brokerage and clearance are separate from the airline bill and depend on the broker, HS code and permit requirements.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Documentation',
    text: 'AWB, commercial invoice, packing list, certificate of origin and any product-specific permit work all add handling time and fees.',
  },
];

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "What is air freight from China to Oman?",
    a: "Air freight from China to Oman typically moves on the $4.3–$11.0/kg corridor band in 3–7 days to Muscat International (MCT), billed on chargeable weight — the higher of actual and volumetric weight. It is used when speed, shelf life or value density justify the higher cost.",
  },
  {
    q: "How is air freight charged — actual weight or volumetric weight?",
    a: "Air freight is billed on the higher of actual gross weight and volumetric weight, where volumetric weight is L × W × H (cm) ÷ 6,000. A light, bulky carton can therefore be billed at far more than its actual weight, while a dense carton is billed at actual weight.",
  },
  {
    q: "How do I calculate volumetric weight for Oman air freight?",
    a: "Volumetric weight is L × W × H (cm) ÷ 6,000, so an 80 × 50 × 40 cm carton is 160,000 cm³ ÷ 6,000 = 26.7 kg. If its actual weight is 20 kg, you pay for 26.7 kg — a 33.5% uplift. Couriers use ÷ 5,000, which makes the same carton 32 kg — a 60% uplift.",
  },
  {
    q: "What does air freight from China to Oman cost?",
    a: "The verified generic China→GCC corridor band is $4.3–$11.0/kg (MEDIUM confidence) and express is $6.9–$11.0/kg. A country-specific Oman per-kg benchmark is not published in the verified snapshot, so request an itemised quote for your actual lane, dimensions and weight rather than relying on a borrowed figure.",
  },
  {
    q: "How long does air freight from China to Muscat take?",
    a: "A planning range of 3–7 days is reasonable for direct airport-to-airport moves. Express courier is also 3–7 days door-to-door, and DDP air is ~10–15 days because it adds destination clearance and final delivery. Airport-specific days are not published — confirm the pair and transit point with your carrier.",
  },
  {
    q: "Which Oman airport should I use for air freight?",
    a: "Muscat International (MCT) is the Oman arrival gateway for China air cargo, sitting under the same $4.3–$11.0/kg, 3–7 day corridor band — match the arrival airport to your consignee and confirm the final-mile leg, because MCT's storage and handling terms are not published.",
  },
  {
    q: "When should I choose air freight instead of sea freight for Oman?",
    a: "Air wins on time — 3–7 days versus 14–30 by sea — while sea wins on volume, since FCL beats LCL above ~15 CBM with a 20ft at ≈ 33 CBM and a 40ft at ≈ 67 CBM. Choose air when the cargo is urgent, high-value, perishable, fragile or needed to avoid a stockout; choose sea when volume is large.",
  },
  {
    q: "Does air freight to Oman save me duty or VAT?",
    a: "No. Air freight shortens transit but does not waive the 5% CIF import duty or the 5% VAT (since 16 April 2021). That tax position applies equally to sea freight — it is not an air-specific benefit.",
  },
  {
    q: "Is SABER or SASO certification required for Oman air freight?",
    a: "No — SABER/SASO is a Saudi-only conformity programme, so Oman clearance instead runs on a 5% CIF duty and 5% VAT basis. This is a common GCC-wide assumption that is wrong. Oman instead applies a 10-digit HS classification, import declaration via the Directorate General of Customs, and product-specific permits for regulated categories.",
  },
  {
    q: "What documents and taxes apply to an Oman air freight import?",
    a: "An Oman air freight import carries 5% CIF duty plus 5% VAT — a ×1.1025 landed-cost multiplier — with an air waybill, commercial invoice, packing list, certificate of origin and correct 10-digit HS classification. De minimis is not published — confirm with the Directorate General of Customs before shipping low-value goods.",
  },
  {
    q: "What hidden fees should I expect on Oman air freight?",
    a: "Beyond the per-kg rate: fuel and security surcharges, terminal handling, airport storage after free time, customs clearance and brokerage, and documentation. Specific amounts were not published in the verified snapshot, so request an itemised schedule for every line.",
  },
  {
    q: "Why is Sohar relevant to an air freight decision?",
    a: "Sohar matters to air only through the sea comparison — 3–7 days by air versus 14–30 days by sea — because it is Oman's primary container gateway outside the Strait of Hormuz and is less congested than Jebel Ali or Jeddah. It does not change the air invoice, but the sea leg you are weighing against air may clear faster on some lanes.",
  },
];

const organization = {
  '@id': `${SITE_URL}/#organization`,
  name: 'WorldFreightHub',
  url: SITE_URL,
  logo: {
    '@type': 'ImageObject',
    url: `${SITE_URL}/favicon.svg`,
  },
};

const schema = [
  {
    type: 'Article',
    data: {
      headline:
        'Air Freight from China to Oman: MCT/Sohar/Salalah Gateways, Chargeable Weight & Rates (September 2026 Updated)',
      description:
        'Air freight from China to Oman: MCT/Sohar/Salalah gateway context, the 6,000-divisor chargeable weight rule, the generic $4.3–$11.0/kg corridor band, 3–7 day transit planning, hidden charges and Oman compliance.',
      datePublished: '2026-09-04',
      dateModified: '2026-09-04',
      inLanguage: 'en',
      mainEntityOfPage: {
        '@type': 'WebPage',
        '@id': pageUrl,
      },
      author: {
        '@type': 'Organization',
        name: 'WorldFreightHub',
        url: SITE_URL,
      },
      publisher: {
        '@id': `${SITE_URL}/#organization`,
      },
      image: `${SITE_URL}/og.png`,
    },
  },
  {
    type: 'FAQPage',
    data: {
      mainEntity: faqs.map((faq) => ({
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: faq.q,
        acceptedAnswer: {
          '@type': 'Answer',
          text: faq.a,
        },
      })),
    },
  },
  {
    type: 'Organization',
    data: organization,
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Air Freight from China to Oman: MCT/Sohar/Salalah Gateways, Chargeable Weight & Rates (September 2026 Updated)"
  description="Air freight from China to Oman: MCT/Sohar/Salalah gateway context, the 6,000-divisor chargeable weight rule, the generic $4.3–$11.0/kg corridor band, 3–7 day transit planning, hidden charges and Oman compliance."
  canonical={pageUrl}
  ogType="article"
  schema={schema}
>
  <div class="page-header">
    <div class="container">
      <Breadcrumb
        items={[
          { label: 'Middle East', href: '/middleeast/' },
          { label: 'Air freight from China to Oman' },
        ]}
      />
      <span class="badge badge--amber">September 2026 updated</span>
      <h1>Air freight from China to Oman: MCT/Sohar/Salalah gateways, chargeable weight &amp; rates (September 2026 updated)</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Air freight to Muscat is fast, but the invoice depends on a number most shippers never
        measure: chargeable weight. This page explains the 6,000-divisor rule that makes bulky
        cargo expensive, the MCT/Sohar/Salalah gateway context, the generic corridor per-kg band,
        3–7 day transit planning, and Oman's 5% duty + 5% VAT position — including why SABER/SASO
        does not apply.
      </p>
      <blockquote class="tldr">
        <p><strong>TL;DR:</strong> Air to Oman (Muscat MCT) runs the $4.3–$11.0/kg corridor band at 3–7 days against 14–30 days by sea — a 2–10× speed gap, but Oman's own per-kg benchmark is not published, so the lane rate must be quoted. Chargeable weight is the first breakpoint: the page's 80 × 50 × 40 cm carton bills at 26.7 kg on ÷6,000 or 32 kg on ÷5,000 versus 20 kg actual — a 33.5% to 60% billable-kg uplift. Oman's 5% duty + 5% VAT is the same ×1.1025 landed-cost multiplier as the UAE, so a $10,000 CIF shipment adds $1,025 in duty and VAT — making the air premium a pure time-value call.</p>
      </blockquote>
      <p class="small muted">
        Confidence badges separate verified figures from indicative planning context. Treat every
        LOW-confidence rate and routing note as indicative, and request an itemised quote for
        unpublished lanes. Oman has no country-specific air benchmark in the verified snapshot.
      </p>

      <div class="fact-grid mt-3">
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">Generic air rate</span>
          <span class="fact-value">$4.3 – $11.0/kg <ConfidenceBadge level="MEDIUM" /></span>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">Oman air rate</span>
          <span class="fact-value">Not published <ConfidenceBadge level="LOW" /></span>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">Air transit</span>
          <span class="fact-value">3–7 days <ConfidenceBadge level="MEDIUM" /></span>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">Chargeable divisor</span>
          <span class="fact-value">÷6000 (air) / ÷5000 (courier) <ConfidenceBadge level="MEDIUM" /></span>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">MCT</span>
          <span class="fact-value">Muscat International <ConfidenceBadge level="LOW" /></span>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">Oman VAT</span>
          <span class="fact-value">5% <ConfidenceBadge level="HIGH" /></span>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">Baseline duty</span>
          <span class="fact-value">5% CIF <ConfidenceBadge level="HIGH" /></span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>1. Air freight rates from China to Oman: what is published and what is not</h2>
    <p>
      Air pricing moves weekly with fuel, capacity, season and airport pairing, so this page
      deliberately separates the verified generic corridor band from the country-specific lane.
      Oman has <strong>no published per-kg air benchmark</strong> in the verified snapshot, so that
      cell is shown as “not published” rather than padded with a borrowed Saudi or UAE figure.
    </p>

    <DataTable
      columns={[
        { key: 'service', label: 'Air service' },
        { key: 'benchmark', label: 'Indicative benchmark' },
        { key: 'transit', label: 'Indicative transit' },
        { key: 'basis', label: 'Basis' },
        { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
      ]}
      rows={airRateRows}
      caption="The generic China→GCC air band is MEDIUM confidence from the methods data. Oman country-specific and DDP per-kg rates were not published — request a quote."
    />
    <SourceList sources={rateSources} title="Sources — Oman air rates" />

    <h3 class="mt-3">Sea context for the “should you fly or sail” decision</h3>
    <p>
      Sea freight is the comparison that decides whether air is worth it. The FCL/LCL economics
      below are MEDIUM confidence from the methods data; the Oman-lane sea rate itself is not
      published, so request it before comparing landed costs. See the full
      <a href="/middleeast/air-freight-china-to-gcc/">air freight China to GCC</a> guide for the
      corridor-level picture and <a href="/middleeast/fcl-vs-lcl/">FCL vs LCL</a> for the ~15 CBM
      crossover logic.
    </p>

    <DataTable
      columns={[
        { key: 'item', label: 'Sea context' },
        { key: 'value', label: 'Verified value' },
        { key: 'basis', label: 'Basis' },
        { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
      ]}
      rows={seaContextRows}
      caption="FCL/LCL rates, the ~15 CBM crossover, sea transit and demurrage are MEDIUM confidence. The Oman sea rate itself is not published — request a quote."
    />
    <SourceList sources={[wfhMethodData]} title="Sources — sea comparison context" />

    <div class="note mt-2">
      <strong>Do not average the rows together.</strong> The $4.3–$11.0/kg figure is a generic
      China→GCC band on an unpublished chargeable-weight base. Compare quotes on total chargeable
      kilograms and the all-in line items, never on the headline per-kg rate alone.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>2. Typical air transit windows from China to Muscat</h2>
      <p>
        The Oman corridor is short enough that air transit is measured in days, not weeks. The
        planning ranges below cover direct airport-to-airport moves, express services and
        door-to-door DDP air; all depend on schedule, customs and the final-mile leg.
      </p>

      <DataTable
        columns={[
          { key: 'method', label: 'Method' },
          { key: 'window', label: 'Transit window' },
          { key: 'note', label: 'What drives the window' },
          { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
        ]}
        rows={airTransitRows}
        caption="Air and express windows are MEDIUM confidence from the methods data; DDP air is LOW confidence. Airport-specific days are not published."
      />
      <SourceList sources={transitSources} title="Sources — air transit times" />

      <div class="note mt-2">
        <strong>Airport pair matters.</strong> The China gateways (PVG/SZX/CAN/HKG) and Muscat
        International (MCT) are operational routing context, not verified in the snapshot — confirm
        the airport pair and transit point with your carrier before relying on a specific day count.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>3. Air vs sea vs express for Oman: the decision framework</h2>
    <p>
      Oman's tax position is a mid-cost point on the corridor: <strong>5% duty on CIF value plus 5%
      VAT</strong> (since 16 April 2021) — lower than Saudi Arabia's 15% VAT or Bahrain's 10% VAT,
      but higher than the UAE's single 5% VAT. That saving applies to both modes, so the decision is
      still speed versus per-unit cost, with the chargeable-weight rule deciding how wide the gap
      really is. See <a href="/middleeast/sea-vs-air-freight-china-to-gcc/">sea vs air freight China
      to GCC</a> for the corridor-wide trade-offs.
    </p>

    <DataTable
      columns={[
        { key: 'factor', label: 'Factor' },
        { key: 'air', label: 'Air freight' },
        { key: 'sea', label: 'Sea freight' },
        { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
      ]}
      rows={airVsSeaRows}
      caption="Transit and pricing are indicative; the ~15 CBM crossover, FCL/LCL rates and demurrage are MEDIUM confidence. The 5% duty and 5% VAT apply equally to both modes."
    />
    <SourceList sources={comparisonSources} title="Sources — air vs sea for Oman" />

    <h3 class="mt-3">When the air premium pays off</h3>
    <div class="grid grid--2 mt-2">
      <Card title="Choose air freight when...">
        <ul>
          <li>Cargo is perishable or has a short shelf life.</li>
          <li>A stockout costs more than the freight premium.</li>
          <li>Goods are high-value per kilogram, so freight is a small share of value.</li>
          <li>Spare parts must reach a stopped operation quickly.</li>
        </ul>
      </Card>
      <Card title="Choose sea freight when...">
        <ul>
          <li>Volume is large — FCL beats LCL above ~15 CBM.</li>
          <li>Cargo is dense, heavy or low-value.</li>
          <li>Sohar's outside-Strait-of-Hormuz resilience is worth capturing on the slower mode.</li>
          <li>You can plan around 14–30 day FCL transit without a stockout.</li>
        </ul>
      </Card>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid--2 mt-2">
      <Card title="Choose express courier when...">
        <ul>
          <li>Parcels are small, urgent and door-to-door tracking matters.</li>
          <li>One booking is worth more than managing an airport-to-airport move.</li>
          <li>You want the courier to handle clearance.</li>
          <li>You accept the ÷5000 divisor for bulky light parcels.</li>
        </ul>
      </Card>
    </div>

    <div class="note mt-2">
      <strong>The tax angle is honest, not a shortcut.</strong> Oman's 5% VAT is lower than
      Bahrain's 10% and Saudi Arabia's 15%, but it does not favour air over sea. If the shipment is
      cost-sensitive and can wait, that saving is a reason to choose the slower sea option — and
      Sohar's congestion advantage can make the sea leg more attractive still.
    </div>
  </section>

