  <section class="section container">
    <h2>7. Compliance: duty, VAT, HS codes and the SABER/SASO differentiator</h2>

      <h3>Duty and VAT</h3>
      <p>
        Oman's baseline import duty is <strong>5% on CIF value</strong>, with higher rates on
        alcohol (100%), cement (50%) and paints &amp; polyurethane (15%), and duty-free treatment for
        basic food and GCC-origin goods. Import VAT is <strong>5%</strong> (since 16 April 2021).
        De minimis is <strong>not published</strong>; confirm with the Directorate General of
        Customs. See <a href="/middleeast/oman-customs-duty/">Oman customs &amp; import duty</a> for
        the full clearance workflow.
      </p>

      <h3>SABER/SASO is Saudi-only</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>SABER/SASO is a Saudi conformity programme and does not apply to Oman.</strong> This
        is a key differentiator: shippers who assume GCC-wide SABER is required end up chasing
        certificates they do not need. Oman instead applies a 10-digit HS classification, import
        declaration via the Directorate General of Customs, product-specific permits for regulated
        categories, and a commercial invoice plus air waybill.
      </p>

      <h3>Documents and classification</h3>
      <p>
        The standard document set is the <strong>air waybill</strong>, commercial invoice, packing
        list and certificate of origin, with the correct 10-digit HS code. Classify before quoting
        because duty and any permit requirements depend on the code, and keep the air waybill
        reference ready for the clearance file.
      </p>

      <p class="small muted">
        Duty, VAT, HS classification and Sohar/Salalah facts on this page are HIGH confidence from
        the WorldFreightHub GCC country and port data; airport roles are LOW confidence operational
        routing context.
      </p>

      <SourceList sources={complianceSources} title="Sources — Oman compliance" />
  </section>

